<H3><CENTER><FONT COLOR="#ffff00"><A HREF="mailto:Mookistar@yahoo.com">Mike Jukes</A></FONT></CENTER></H3>

        <BR><BR>
	Mike is now on his second sedicivalvole,  thanks to a
	BMW and a very confusing accident writing off his old
	black 3 door. Immediately after the accident,  his
	fiance' offered to lend him the funds to get a new
	car,  and his immediate choice was another Tipo 16v.
	By chance he had seen just a week earlier an  L reg 3
	door, finished in Estoril blue, in the autotrader,
	and gave the guy a call.
	<P>
	As soon as he could he went all the way to Leicester
	to test drive it and came away very impressed.
	However despite offering money there and then,  the
	owner had given first refusal to somebody else.
	Mike had 7 days of hell,  waiting to see if the car
	would sell,  but it was worth it,  as he got a call to
	say the other guy had changed his mind.
	<P>
	The car needed a few obligatory jobs doing to it,  so
	despite driving it home,  he didn't drive the car for
	a week as he waited for ALL the belts to be replaced.
	A wise move as it happened as the cambelt,  deposit
	being changed just 6,000 miles earlier,  was extremely
	loose,  and could have jumped at any minute.
	New disks and belts later and Mike was the proud owner
	of a 1994 L registration, 3 door sedici with 68,000
	miles on the clock.  The car is totally standard with
	the newer 3 door style wheels and usual interior and
	exterior styling,  apart from the addition of a very
	tidy boot spoiler.
	<P>
	About 5 months after the black sedici accident,  Mike
	received a 50/50 pay out of &pound;1100 and got to keep his
	black sedici.  Which he stripped with the help of
	George Roberts. So far he has fitted the exhaust from
	his old car, a brake caliper, the windscreen wiper
	assembly along with many other smaller bits.  He has
	easily saved the &pound;500 or so pounds offered for his old
	car by breakers by using parts from his old car,  and
	of course he still has a spare engine and gearbox, 2
	doors, radiator and a whole host of the usual dodgy
	Fiat electric components.
	<P>
	Since owning the New Sedici,  he has fitted a K&N 57i
	induction kit, with increased  ventilation, a
	Superchips chip and uprated shocks. Overall Mike is
	very happy with his sedici despite catching the Turbo
	bug after advising his Fiance to buy a Punto GT Turbo,
	however he intends to keep his 16v for a few years
	yet.
	<P>
	Mike recently fitted Fiat Coupe front seats in his car.
	<P>
        Mikes old Tipo was a 3 door black 93 L registration
        Tipo 16v. Mike is 20 years old and lives in Chingford, East London.
        Mike says that the car is an absolute dream to drive, and hasn't
        found anything else on the road that can even touch it for
        performance and subtlty. Currently the car has a direct replacement
        K&amp;N filter and a Starchip which Mike claims produces
        about 155 bhp. Mike has only had the car for about 4 months
        (April 1999) but says he finds the handling a little bit
        &quot;boaty&quot;.
        <P>
        Mike loves the subtlty of the car. He recently had an XR2 in front
        of him, and they thought he was trying to race them.  As they
        pulled away with all their might,  Mike pulled out and
        went straight past them. At the next set of lights, side by side,
        the passenger winds down his window, and asks Mike what the
        F#@K the car is!! Mike has raced his brothers Citro&euml;n ZX
        2.0 16v and it couldn't keep up with him. However his other brothers
        3.0 Carlton CDX is a bit quicker after 60mph.
        <P>
        Mike had only had the car for 3 days before he took it a gentle
        drive around the roads of Epping and Abridge. He knew these roads
        very well. However, not being completely familiar with the Tipo he
        was taking it easy.
        <P>
        Certain fields along this route have very poor drainage. Water
        therefore tends to run over the surface of the road throughout the
        year. Unfortunately two months of winter weather had wreaked havoc
        by stripping away parts of the tarmac exposing the original concrete
        surface.
        <P>
        As Mike approached the corner at 40 mph he unfortunately managed
        to dab the brakes just as the wheels hit the pools of standing
        water that had formed causing the car to aquaplane. Before he could
        even think about pumping the brake pedal (no ABS) he was on the
        grass. The car hit three boulders that lined a driveway before
        stopping two inches away from a telegraph pole.
        <P>
        The front bumper was cracked in two. The radiator now an interesting
        V-shape as all its mountings had been bent. The oil filter and all of
        its oil were all over the place. The front sub-frame cross member
        now had added contours. The rear exhaust system was beaten into a
        new and much noisier shape. There was a hole the size of Mike's fist
        in the passenger floor compartment. An alloy wheel and associated
        tyre looked rather beaten up. One of the side skirts was smashed
        up and there was a hole in the gearbox.
        <P>
        To get it back on the road Mike needed oil and an oil filter. A new
        radiator and sub-frame. The gearbox was repaired as it was a pin
        hole fracture. The exhaust still works and the body work is still
        cracked. This lot cost &pound;700 just for roadworthiness.
        However Mike is suing the council for the accident (good luck).
        With a new bumper, side skirts and exhaust the total damage will
        be more like &pound;2500. Since the accident the road has been
        resurfaced and the drainage sorted out.

<P>
